  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows a deep, closed mid/upper low centered\nover southern Utah/northern Arizona with attendant troughing \nextending southward into the Gulf of California and \nsouthwesterly mid/upper flow downstream across the Plains and\ninto Missouri. At the surface, the cold front is draped from NE\nMissouri to the southwest through Kansas City and into eastern\nKansas. Stratiform rain continues across southern portions of\nthe CWA this morning, with new convection beginning to develop\nacross east central Kansas from Salina to Emporia, moving slowly\nto the east. With the diminished threat for additional heavy \nrainfall this morning, the flood watch has been canceled for\nalmost the entire CWA, except for Linn County KS and Bates and\nHenry County MO (more on this later). The front should continue\nto slowly push southward through the day today, with low stratus\nremaining and much cooler temperatures expected (mid to upper \n60s for most locations). \n\nIsolated to scattered elevated showers/storms north of the \nfront will be possible by late morning into early afternoon. The\naforementioned western trough is projected to move eastward \nacross the Southern Rockies and into the Southern Plains by \nlater today, finally ejecting across the central CONUS overnight\ntonight into Thursday morning. The associated surface cyclone \nshould lift out of the TX/OK Panhandles and into eastern KS \novernight tonight into early Thursday, which will draw the cold \nfront back to the north as a warm front. All of this should help\ngenerate widespread showers (with some embedded thunderstorms) \nacross the CWA late tonight into Thursday morning, and with\nPWATs projected to range from 1.8\" to 2\", it is possible that \nfar southern portions of the CWA could pick up 1-2\" or so of \nrain with this event, so the aforementioned flood watch for \nLinn, Bates, and Henry Counties has been extended out through \nThursday afternoon. Temperatures on Thursday should be warmer as\nthe warm front moves south to north through the CWA, with highs\nin the mid 70s for most locations, but as the surface low exits\nto the northeast, the trailing cold front should move through\nfrom west to east Thursday afternoon/evening. Winds will turn\nnorthwesterly behind the front, with sustained winds on the\norder of 15 to 20 mph and gusts up to 25 to 30 mph, as well as\nmuch cooler temperatures and lower dew points. Low temperatures\nFriday morning should be in the mid 40s across the region, with\nbreezy conditions yielding wind chills in the mid to upper 30s.\nWith strong cold air advection on Friday, afternoon highs should\nonly reach the mid to upper 50s, with breezy northerly winds\ncontinuing through mid to late afternoon.\n\nThe pressure gradient should begin to subside by Friday evening \nas a 1036 mb surface high slides eastward out of the central\nHigh Plains and toward our CWA. By Friday night, the surface \nhigh should be centered directly overhead, yielding calm winds, \nclear skies, and low dew points. This will make for excellent \nradiational cooling conditions, and temperatures early Saturday \nmorning should fall into the mid to upper 30s across the region \n(warmer of course across the KC urban head island). This should \nyield some patchy frost across the area, especially northern \nMissouri where temperatures should be the coldest. Saturday \nshould be a beautiful mid October day, with highs in the low to \nmid 60s, mostly clear skies, and light winds. Mid level ridging \nbegins to build in from the west by Sunday, with deep layer \nsouthwesterly flow moving in by late Sunday into Monday \ndownstream of a trough over Baja California. This should bring \nin warmer temperatures and increased moisture, with highs back \ninto the low to mid 70s across most of the CWA by next Monday \nafternoon.",
